# CONSULT-64 — dPVS library internals + frame-to-frame state angle (Sonnet)

Scope: vendored dPVS source only (src/external/3rd/library/dpvs/), occlusion-buffer path
already eliminated per evidence pack (occlusionMode=off). Mechanism only, no fixes proposed.

## LEAD FINDING — near-zero-epsilon clip-plane classification can reject a whole portal
## transition, independent of renderer/bitness, and self-heals on a tiny camera move

Call chain for every portal traversal (occlusion off):
`VisibilityQuery::traversePortal()` [dpvsVisibilityQuery_Traverse.cpp:512]
  -> `p->getTestRectangle(portalRectangle)` [dpvsImpObject.cpp:1230] — for a portal this ALWAYS
     calls `m_testModel->getExactRectangle(r)` (never the plain OBB rect — "portals are so
     expensive we always want exact rectangles")
  -> `ImpMeshModel::getExactRectangle()` [dpvsImpMeshModel.cpp:540] first calls
     `SilhouetteMath::getClippedRectangle_FLOW()` -> `getClippedRectangle()`
     [dpvsSilhouetteMath.cpp:588].

`getClippedRectangle()` clips the portal's OBB (6 quad faces) against the CURRENT camera's clip
planes via `clipPolygonPlane()` [dpvsClipPolygon.cpp:40]. That function classifies each vertex
with a **hard, zero-tolerance comparison**:

```cpp
clipVal[i] = -(polygon[i].x*plEq.x + polygon[i].y*plEq.y + polygon[i].z*plEq.z + plEq.w);
if (clipVal[i] < 0.0f)   // should this be <= ?     <-- original dPVS author's own doubt, in-source
    allClipped = false;
```

There is no epsilon anywhere in this test. If EVERY vertex of EVERY one of the 6 OBB faces
lands with `clipVal <= 0.0f` for that face's governing plane comparison (`allClipped==true` for
all 6, or every face's clip degenerates to 0 vertices), `getClippedRectangle()` hits
`if (!valid) return false;` [line 743] — the "camera is not inside the OBB and OBB is outside
the screen" path. This propagates: `getExactRectangle()` returns false -> `getTestRectangle()`
returns false -> `traversePortal()` returns false at line 558-559 -> the ENTIRE downstream
transition (portal, target cell, and everything reachable only through it) is **rejected for
this frame's query** — no assertion, no error, just silently not traversed. This is upstream of
render-queue submission, exactly matching the RenderDoc evidence ("missing cells' geometry
never submitted").

There is a second fail-closed path in the same function: even when some faces DO clip validly,
if the accumulated depth range degenerates (`mnz > mxz` after clamping, line 750-751) the
function also returns false. Both paths are reachable specifically when the portal's (thin,
often near-planar) OBB straddles a clip plane at a shallow/grazing angle — i.e. exactly the
geometric situation of a camera standing at/near a doorway threshold, looking through it at a
shallow angle. (There is also a companion front-clip branch, lines 703-725, that substitutes the
FULL VIEWPORT rectangle instead of rejecting — that path is fail-OPEN, not relevant to the
missing-geometry symptom, but shows the same file has two different escape hatches for the same
front-clipping condition, in tension with each other.)

Why this matches the locked symptom set precisely:
- **Renderer-agnostic + bitness-agnostic**: this is pure dPVS C++ float math (object/camera
  matrices, dot products), executed identically regardless of gl05/D3D9 vs gl11/D3D11, and
  regardless of Win32 vs x64 — it never touches a graphics API. The historical
  D3DCREATE_FPU_PRESERVE fix addressed a DIFFERENT precision corruption (x87 control-word
  clamping from the D3D9 runtime affecting shared portal-visibility math broadly); this
  mechanism is dPVS's own zero-epsilon comparison and is agnostic to that fix, consistent with
  the evidence pack's note that the current bug reproduces on x64/SSE (no x87 clamping) as "a
  different instance, though possibly the same math neighborhood."
- **"Standing at/near the cantina entrance"**: the portal quad's OBB is only marginally on one
  side of a view-frustum clip plane at exactly this position/angle range.
- **"A SMALL camera angle/position change restores correct rendering instantly"**: any
  perturbation of camera position/orientation changes every dot product in
  `clipPolygonPlane()` by a tiny but non-exactly-zero amount, flipping one or more vertices away
  from the exact classification tie. No state persists across frames in this path — it is
  recomputed fresh from the current camera pose every query — so this also explains "self-
  clearing; no persistence."
- **Variable magnitude of missing draws** (Pair A: 42 draws missing; Pair B: 160 draws missing):
  rejection higher up the portal-recursion chain (a doorway that gates a large sub-graph of
  cells) removes proportionally more geometry than rejection of a leaf portal — consistent with
  two different-sized failures being observed from the same root mechanism.

This is a single, self-contained, deterministic-given-camera-pose mechanism entirely inside the
vendored dPVS math, requiring no cross-frame state, no engine-side bug, and no occlusion buffer.
It is the most parsimonious explanation of the RenderDoc evidence and should be the first thing
probed at the actual sighting location (e.g. instrument `getClippedRectangle()`'s `!valid` and
`mnz>mxz` return points for the cantina entrance portal object, or watch `clipVal[]` values for
its OBB corners as camera position is nudged through the reported trigger zone).

## Task 1 — portal traversal path, camera-cell assignment, frame-coherency state

- **Camera-cell assignment**: `VisibilityQuery::launchTravelers()` [Traverse.cpp:52] takes the
  camera's cell via `m_currentCamera->getCell()` UNCONDITIONALLY, once, at the start of the
  query (line 168: `DPVS_ASSERT (c->getCell())`). dPVS does not re-derive or validate which cell
  the camera "actually" belongs to — that is entirely the CALLER's (engine's) responsibility via
  `Camera::setCell()`/similar, invoked once per query setup. If the engine ever hands dPVS a
  stale/wrong cell pointer for the camera, dPVS will traverse that cell's portal graph with zero
  self-correction — but this is an ENGINE-side integration question (out of this angle's scope;
  the other consultants tracing the call graph should confirm whether/when the engine
  re-resolves camera-cell containment per frame vs. caching it).
- **Portal traversal termination conditions** (`RecursionSolver`, dpvsRecursionSolver.cpp):
  three independent gates, each of which silently drops a transition (returns `false` from
  `testTransition()`, Traverse.cpp:616) without any diagnostic:
  1. `testRecursionDepth()` — hard depth limit (`m_maximumDepth`, set once via
     `Camera::setRecursionDepth()` — engine-configured, likely small, e.g. 3-8). Keyed by a
     `Hash<Transition,int>` where the KEY equality is the FULL (not truncated) 64-bit
     `(m_s,m_d)` pointer pair (`Transition::operator==`, dpvsRecursionSolver.hpp:85) — collisions
     in the (deliberately truncated-to-32-bit, x64-safe per in-source comment) HASH BUCKET are
     resolved correctly via full key compare, so this is NOT a correctness bug on x64, only a
     (harmless) bucket-distribution one. See "x64/pointer" section below for the full note.
  2. `testReTraversal()` — rejects a SECOND `REAL_TRANSITION` into a cell already reached this
     query via a different portal chain (prevents duplicate-cell processing / infinite loops on
     cyclic portal graphs). This is a legitimate one-arrival-per-cell-per-query rule, not a bug,
     but it means: if a cell is reachable via two distinct real portals from the entry room
     (e.g. two doorways/windows into the same interior), only the FIRST one processed (by
     traversal order, see PQueue note below) actually contributes its frustum; the second is
     dropped outright. Combined with traversal-order instability (below), this can make the
     *narrower* of two valid portal views "win" on one frame and the *wider* one win on the
     next, producing exactly a "wrong/tighter clip" flicker at a doorway with redundant portal
     geometry — a secondary candidate mechanism worth checking if the cantina entrance in fact
     has more than one physical-portal path between the same two cells.
  3. Importance-threshold decay (`m_importanceThreshold`, Traverse.cpp:640-651) — if the engine
     sets a non-zero importance threshold, cumulative decay along a longer/indirect traversal
     path can drop a transition that would otherwise succeed via a more direct path. Order-
     dependent (same traversal-order sensitivity as #2).
- **Frame-coherency / temporal caching that DOES exist in the library** (all found, all
  candidates for task 1's "frame coherency" question):
  - `Node::m_lastVisible` / `Node::m_lastOcclusionTestVisible` — Jiri Bittner node-skipping +
    visible-point-tracking caches (Database.cpp `traverseNode()` ~line 3052, `isNodeVisible()`
    ~line 2635). **Confirmed inert under occlusionMode=off**: `isNodeVisible()` line 2645-2646
    returns `true` unconditionally the instant `!VQData::get().performOcclusionCulling()` — so
    with occlusion off, this cache path never diverges from "always visible" and cannot be the
    cause. This independently CONFIRMS (does not just assume) the evidence pack's elimination of
    the occlusion-buffer subsystem, at the Node-cache level as well as the buffer-instantiation
    level.
  - `ImpCamera::setTimeStamp(++s_portalTimeStamp)` on every synthesized child camera
    (Traverse.cpp:572) — a monotonically increasing global counter, used only for cache-
    invalidation bookkeeping elsewhere (write-queue / occlusion writer); not implicated while
    occlusion is off.
  - `Database::m_timeStamp` (incremented once per `traverse()` call, i.e. once per cell-query)
    and `ImpObject`'s per-object timestamp — used to prevent re-processing the same object twice
    within one query (multi-node object instances). Purely per-query dedup; resets cleanly every
    call. Not a cross-frame persistence hazard.
  - **`Database::m_updateHead`/`m_updateTail` dirty-node list + `optimizeHiddenAreas()`
    background job** — this is real, PERSISTENT, budget-throttled cross-frame state. See Task 2.

## Task 2 — what settles asynchronously after zone-in (dPVS's own answer)

Each `CellProperty`/cell owns its own independent dPVS `IDatabase` (spatial BSP-like node tree)
— confirmed at `ImpCell` construction, `m_database(NEW<IDatabase>())` (dpvsImpCell.cpp:56).
`DPVS_HIERARCHICAL_DATABASE` (voxel/node splitting) **is compiled in** for this build — it is
defined by default in `dpvsPrivateDefs.hpp:768` unless `DPVS_STABLE` is defined, and `DPVS_STABLE`
is not defined anywhere in the tree (grep confirmed, only referenced at its own `#ifndef` site).
So node splitting/collapsing is active, not disabled.

The relevant self-throttling machinery, all in `dpvsDatabase.cpp`:
- `Database::traverse()` [~line 3511] computes `optimizationBudget` as **2.5% of the time just
  spent on this frame's actual visibility traversal** (`optimizationBudget *= 0.025`,
  line 3580), then spends that budget in `optimizeHiddenAreas(optimizationBudget)`.
- `optimizeHiddenAreas()` [line 914] additionally caps itself to `m_numNodes>>6` (i.e. ~1/64 of
  the tree's nodes) per call regardless of remaining time budget.
- `updateDirtyNode()` [line 2299] is where the actual work happens: splitting an over-full node
  (`splitNode`+`pushObjectsDown`, when a node exceeds `MIN_NODE_INSTANCE_COUNT`=12 instances),
  collapsing an under-full one, and recomputing `m_tightBounds`/`m_visiblePoint` for LEAF nodes
  only (non-leaf nodes never get tight bounds — their frustum test always falls back to the
  wider, static, split-derived `m_bounds`, which fails OPEN not closed, so that particular
  staleness is not itself a missing-geometry vector).
- Any newly-added/moved object marks its node (and ancestors, via `setNodeDirty`/
  `setParentAccumulatedCostDirty`) dirty; dirty nodes are re-linked to the TAIL of the update
  list (`linkNodeToUpdateTail`), so a fresh burst of insertions (zone-in populating an interior
  cell with furniture/NPCs/dynamic objects all at once) can take **many frames** to fully drain
  through this 2.5%-budget, 1/64-node-count-capped background job before the tree structure
  (and per-leaf tight bounds / visible points) is fully "settled."

This is dPVS's own documented mechanism for exactly the kind of "still settling minutes after
cell creation" window the task asked about — it is not a guess, it is the explicit budget
formula in `Database::traverse()`. It is a live candidate independent of the lead finding above:
while a cell's node tree is mid-restructure, `Node::getTestBounds()` for affected nodes may
temporarily use the coarser split-derived `m_bounds` rather than the tight object-derived AABB,
changing which nodes/objects get visited by the priority-queue traversal (see next item) even
though it should not, by itself, cause an already-well-formed node to falsely fail a frustum
test (coarser bounds are always a superset).

**Traversal-order instability (secondary but real, ties into #2 above and into Task 1's
"portal ordering" question):** `Database::traverseNode()` enqueues objects/child-nodes into a
`PQueue<TraversalElement>` [dpvsPriorityQueue.hpp] sorted by
`getSquaredBoxNearDistance()` — the squared distance from the CURRENT camera location to each
object/node's AABB, recomputed fresh every traversal from `VQData::get().getCameraLocation()`
(dpvsDatabase.cpp:256-303). The priority queue is a **plain, non-stable binary heap**: ties
(`m_items[parent] <= m_items[n]`, no swap on equality) resolve by insertion order, and insertion
order into a node's linked list is **LIFO** — `createInstance()` always prepends
(`instance->m_vNext = v->getFirstInstance(); v->setFirstInstance(instance);`,
dpvsDatabase.cpp:1329-1341) — so the most-recently-created object/portal-instance is always
processed first among ties. During/after zone-in, as objects are created asynchronously and the
tree is mid-restructure (previous paragraph), both (a) which objects tie in distance and (b)
their relative creation order are actively churning. Combined with `RecursionSolver::
testReTraversal()`'s "first real transition into a cell wins, second is dropped" rule (Task 1,
item 2), a traversal-order flip between two portals leading to overlapping cells/geometry can
change WHICH portal's (possibly narrower) view frustum ends up gating that geometry, or
whether an importance-threshold decay accumulated along a longer path first causes a legitimate
transition to be dropped. This is consistent with, but secondary to, the lead finding: it
requires a specific topology (redundant portal paths or non-trivial importance threshold) to
manifest, whereas the lead finding (clip-plane classification) requires only ordinary single-
portal geometry at a grazing angle.

## Task 3 — x64/pointer/precision scan (secondary only; bug reproduces on Win32 too)

- `RecursionSolver`'s `Transition` hash function (dpvsRecursionSolver.cpp:37-40) truncates
  pointer values to a 32-bit hash deliberately, per an in-source comment already present
  ("ad-hoc hash function (UPTR: pointer-width-safe on x64, low-32 hash intentional)") — this
  reads as a change already made and reasoned about by a previous investigator/phase. Verified
  harmless for correctness on x64: `Hash<Key,Value>::get()`/`exists()` (dpvsHash.hpp) always
  re-checks full key equality (`table[h].key == s`, i.e. the untruncated 64-bit `Transition::
  operator==` comparing both `m_s` and `m_d` pointers in full) after the hash-bucket lookup, so a
  32-bit truncation collision only costs a slightly longer bucket chain, never a false match.
  **Not a live x64 correctness suspect** (already checked and closed by a prior investigator per
  the in-source annotation; re-verified independently here).
- No other pointer-keyed hash/set structures were found in the portal-traversal-adjacent code
  (`Set<ImpObject*>`, `Hash<Transition,int>` are the only two in this path); neither showed a
  pointer-size assumption that would behave differently under x64 vs Win32.
- Portal geometry transforms (`Matrix4x3`/`Matrix4x4`/`Vector3`/`Vector4`) throughout
  `dpvsImpPhysicalPortal.cpp`, `dpvsSilhouetteMath.cpp`, `dpvsClipPolygon.cpp` are plain `float`
  (32-bit) math end-to-end; no `double`, no platform-conditional precision code, nothing
  `#ifdef`'d on architecture/bitness was found in the portal/clip/frustum path. This matches the
  evidence pack's observation that the bug reproduces on BOTH Win32 and x64 — the lead finding's
  zero-epsilon `clipVal < 0.0f` classification (dpvsClipPolygon.cpp:51) is bitness-agnostic by
  construction (same IEEE-754 float math, same code, no architecture branch), which is why it
  remains the best fit rather than an x64-specific SSE/x87 rounding-difference theory: whatever
  rounding noise flips the classification exists on BOTH architectures (just with different
  noise characteristics), consistent with "reproduces on both, not identical, possibly same math
  neighborhood" already noted in the evidence pack for the historical x87 fix.
- Flag, do not chase further under this task's framing: nothing x64-only was found that could
  cull a whole cell. The one true pointer-hash-truncation spot is already correctness-safe by
  construction (full-key re-check) and already has an in-source note suggesting it was already
  reviewed.

## Summary of candidate mechanisms, ranked

1. **(Lead)** `getClippedRectangle()`'s zero-epsilon `clipPolygonPlane()` vertex classification
   (dpvsClipPolygon.cpp:51) causing `getExactRectangle()`/`getTestRectangle()` to fail for a
   portal's OBB at a grazing viewing angle -> `traversePortal()` rejects the whole downstream
   transition for that frame only. Single-frame, camera-pose-only, no persistent state,
   renderer- and bitness-agnostic. Best fit for all four locked symptom characteristics.
2. **(Secondary, timing)** Per-cell `Database`'s budget-throttled (`2.5%` time, `1/64` node
   count per call) background node-split/collapse job (`optimizeHiddenAreas`/
   `updateDirtyNode`) taking multiple frames to converge after a burst of object insertions at
   zone-in — dPVS's own explicit mechanism matching the "settles within seconds to minutes"
   timing profile, independent of mechanism 1.
3. **(Secondary, order-dependent)** Priority-queue (non-stable heap) traversal-order sensitivity
   to camera position + LIFO object-instance insertion order, interacting with
   `RecursionSolver::testReTraversal()`'s "first arrival wins" rule for cells reachable via
   multiple portals, or with a non-zero importance threshold's cumulative decay. Requires
   redundant-portal topology or non-zero importance threshold to manifest; worth checking engine
   config for `Camera::setImportance`/threshold value and whether the cantina entrance has more
   than one physical portal into the same interior cell.
4. Occlusion-buffer node-visibility cache (`Node::m_lastVisible`/`m_lastOcclusionTestVisible`,
   visible-point tracking) — independently CONFIRMED inert while occlusionMode=off
   (`isNodeVisible()` short-circuits to `true` before touching any cached state). Reinforces,
   does not contradict, the evidence pack's existing elimination of this subsystem.
5. Pointer-hash truncation in `RecursionSolver` — checked, correctness-safe by full-key
   re-verification, already flagged/commented by a prior pass. Not a live suspect.
